      "Narrowest combo frame here, at 34 in.",
      "Rated for home and commercial use",
      "Sold by Body-Solid, dealers and on Amazon",
    ],
    cons: [
      "At 96 in. long, it needs a long wall",
      "Carriage weight differs between Body-Solid (60 lb) and Iron Company (80 lb)",
      "Body-Solid does not publish a height range or shoulder-pad spread",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The combo for lifters who value pad comfort and a narrow frame and have the length to spare.",
    description: "The GLPH1100B is a plate-loaded hack squat and leg press on a quad-track roller system, with flip-and-lock back pads, diamond-plate footplates for both positions, under-carriage plate loading, three lockout positions and a 2 by 4 inch 11-gauge steel mainframe. For hack squats, Body-Solid gives the back and shoulder pads as 4 inches thick, double-stitched and contoured, the most detailed pad description of the combos here. Body-Solid listed it in stock at $2,200 on 19 October and Iron Company at $1,735, and an Amazon listing was indexed. Body-Solid, Iron Company and the Amazon listing agree on the 1,000 lb capacity and three lockouts, and Body-Solid and Iron Company on the 96 by 34 inch footprint; Body-Solid gives a 56 inch height and 317 lb weight. Body-Solid gives the empty carriage as 60 lb and Iron Company as 80 lb, so we have left it off. It ships in five boxes. Body-Solid lists both in-home and commercial warranties for it; check which applies to you.",
  },
  {
    id: "gmwd-pendulum-squat",
    name: "GMWD Pendulum Squat Machine PS01",
    brand: "GMWD",
    category: "Spring-counterbalanced pendulum squat",
    badge: "Best Pendulum",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CVTYVN24?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41EnEJQIqbL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$799.99–$899.99",
    priceBounds: {"low":799.99,"high":899.99},
    specs: "Pendulum lever with removable spring counterbalance · 5 bottom safety-stop positions, 18 to 23.7 in. · about 63 x 43 in. footprint · 287 lb · Olympic plates",
    pros: [
      "A pendulum squat for well under $1,000",
      "A removable spring counterbalance that GMWD says makes the bottom easier and the top harder",
      "Five bottom safety-stop positions, so a failed rep lands on the stopper",
      "Listings and GMWD agree on about a 63 x 43 inch footprint and 287 lb",
    ],
    cons: [
      "GMWD gives a lifetime frame but only six months on parts; one retailer lists two years",
      "Capacity is given as 800 lb by retailers; we found no GMWD figure to match it, so it is not on the card",
      "No in-depth independent review we could find",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For a home lifter who wants the pendulum feel without commercial money, and who will read the warranty terms first.",
    description: "GMWD's PS01 is a plate-loaded pendulum squat: the shoulder pads and back pad ride a lever that swings in an arc, rather than a sled on rails. GMWD's Amazon listing describes a removable spring counterbalance that makes the movement easier at the bottom, where you are weakest, and harder at the top, and a bottom safety stopper with five positions from 18 to 23.7 inches, and gives a 63 by 42 inch footprint and a 287 lb weight; Amazon's own product data and retailer listings give about 63 by 43 by 62 inches and the same 287 lb. Retailer listings give an 800 lb capacity, which we could not match to a GMWD figure. GMWD's store gives a lifetime warranty on the frame and six months on parts, while one retailer lists two years, so get the terms in writing. We saw it at $799.99 on Amazon on 9 November 2026 and at $899.99, against $1,199.99, on GMWD's store, which showed a pre-order note. It takes the slot the Bells of Steel Pandemonium held on this page; Bells of Steel sells the Pandemonium on its own store, not on Amazon.",
  },
  {
    id: "body-solid-leverage-squat-calf",
    name: "Body-Solid Leverage Squat/Calf Machine (GSCL360)",
    brand: "Body-Solid",
    category: "Plate-loaded lever squat and calf raise",
    badge: "Most Commercial",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000M0H268?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31wpbmxRcTL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$999.88–$1,155",
    priceBounds: {"low":999.88,"high":1155},
    specs: "3 x 3 in., 12-gauge steel mainframe · 170 lb · spring-loaded lockout handle · round calf-raise block · contoured shoulder and back pads · Olympic plates",
    pros: [
      "A lever squat on a 3 x 3 in., 12-gauge commercial-style frame",
      "Body-Solid and retailers agree on the 170 lb weight and the frame",
      "A spring-loaded lockout handle within reach, and a calf-raise mode",
      "Sold by home and commercial retailers, and on Amazon",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Body-Solid’s data sheet gives 43 in. wide and its newer listing 47 in., so we left the footprint off the specification line",
      "A lever arc rather than a sled, so it feels different from a classic hack squat",
      "No published user weight limit that we found",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For a home gym that wants commercial-style lever squats and calf raises for about $1,000 to $1,155, after confirming the width.",
    description: "Body-Solid's GSCL360 is a plate-loaded lever machine: shoulder pads on a pivoting arm guide a squat or a standing calf raise in an arc, the way commercial-gym lever squats do. Body-Solid's Amazon listing and its data sheet describe a 3 x 3 inch, 12-gauge high-tensile steel mainframe welded on all four sides, extra-thick contoured shoulder and back pads, a round calf-raise block and a spring-loaded lockout handle, and Body-Solid and several retailers agree on a 170 lb machine weight. The footprint is the one conflict: Body-Solid's data sheet gives 62 x 43 x 56 inches and its current GSCL360B listing 62 x 47 x 56, so measure for 47 inches. One retailer gives a 35 lb carriage weight, which we could not match to a Body-Solid figure. We saw it at $1,155 on Amazon on 9 November 2026, and retailers list it from about $1,000. It takes the slot the Steelflex PLHP Plate Loaded Hack Press held on this page; Steelflex sells through dealers, not on Amazon, and this is the closest commercial-style lever squat we could check that is.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Home Gym", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/home-gym/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Hack Squat Machines", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-hack-squat-machines/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best hack squat machine for a home gym?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a dedicated hack squat, Titan's Plate-Loaded Linear Hack Squat, a 35-degree sled on linear bearings for about $975, though its figures are Titan's own. For a machine that also leg presses, Body-Solid's GLPH1100B, whose capacity, lockouts and footprint Body-Solid and a dealer agree on; Titan's combo is the best-reviewed but is sold on Titan's store rather than on Amazon. For a short room, the Force USA Compact at 30 degrees.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a hack squat or leg press better for a home gym?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on what you want to train. A hack squat loads you through the shoulders and moves your body with the sled, so it feels closer to a squat and is harder with the same plates. A leg press keeps your body still and loads only through the feet. A combo does both in one footprint, which is why most home buyers choose one.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What angle should a hack squat machine be?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "There is no single right angle. Most combos run at about 45 degrees; Titan's dedicated sled is 35 and Force USA's Compact 30. A steeper sled puts more of your body weight and the plates into the lift; a shallower one leans you back and feels lighter. Compare numbers only on the same machine.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does a hack squat feel heavier than a leg press?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On a hack squat your body rides the sled, so you lift a share of your own weight as well as the plates: about 71% of it on a 45-degree sled, ignoring friction. On a leg press your body stays still. Garage Gym Reviews also found the hack squat on Titan's combo heavier than on most commercial machines.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much space does a home hack squat machine need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Titan lists its dedicated hack squat at 46.75 by 60.5 inches, the GMWD PS01 pendulum is about 63 by 43, and the leg press combos are 84 to 96 inches long. Allow about three feet around the frame to step in, load plates and swap pads. Pendulum and lever machines swing upward as you stand, so check the ceiling too.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a hack squat machine safe to use alone?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is one of the safer ways to squat hard alone, provided you set the bottom stop just below your deepest safe position every session, practise the lockout levers with an empty sled, load plates evenly and stay within the user weight limit. Talk to a qualified professional first if you have a knee, back, neck or heart condition or high blood pressure.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-19",
      dateModified: "2026-10-19",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 6,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-hack-squat-machines/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-19",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-19",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best hack squat machine",
      "home hack squat machine",
      "hack squat leg press combo",
      "pendulum squat machine",
      "plate loaded hack squat",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our Top Picks", link: "#picks" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-besthacksquatmachines" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-besthacksquatmachines)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buying Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Hack Squat Machines <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">for a Home Gym</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          A home hack squat can be a dedicated sled, one half of a leg press combo, a pendulum lever or a commercial-style hack press. Here are six on sale now, how the sled angle changes what the plates feel like, and the shoulder-pad, safety-stop, storage and floor checks to make before you buy.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#picks" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            See the Picks
          </a>
          <a href="#sled-angle" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Sled Angle Explained
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 19, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="1.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not tested these
          machines, and no figure on this page is our own measurement. Each was confirmed as
          currently sold on 19 October 2026 on the maker's own store (Titan Fitness, Bells of Steel
          and Body-Solid). On 9 November 2026 we replaced four machines we first carded, the Force
          USA Compact, the Bells of Steel Pandemonium, the Steelflex PLHP and Titan's Leg Press Hack
          Squat combo, which are sold direct
          or through dealers but not on Amazon, with the nearest machines sold on Amazon US, checked
          against the maker's store or Amazon listing and at least one other source. Angle, capacity,
          dimensions and price were checked on that date against at least one independent source
          where one existed — Garage Gym Reviews, Iron Company, indexed Amazon listings and a UK
          retailer. A figure appears in a product's specification line only where the maker and an
          independent source agree; where they conflicted, we left it out and say so on the card.
          Two machines (Titan's dedicated hack squat and the Bells of Steel combo) have little or
          no independent coverage, so their figures are the maker's and are labelled as such. Every
          machine on this page is sold on Amazon US; check the model name and seller before you
          order. Every brand was checked against the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall
          database. Prices are those we saw on that date and change often. We earn a commission if
          you buy through the Amazon links on this page; it does not change which products we list
          or what we say about them.
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What this page is, and is not.</strong> We review home
          exercise equipment; we are not giving medical, physiotherapy, coaching or structural
          engineering advice. Rated capacities are manufacturer figures, not a guide to what you
          should lift. A hack squat loads the knees, hips and spine through the shoulders and
          raises blood pressure under heavy effort; if you have a knee, hip, back, neck or heart
          condition, high blood pressure, are pregnant or postpartum, or are returning from injury
          or surgery, talk to a qualified professional before starting or changing a programme.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>


    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <div class="not-prose bg-orange-50 border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-3">Our Top Picks</h2>
        <ul class="list-none m-0 p-0">
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Best Dedicated Hack Squat:</span>
