        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Hack Squat Machines</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines. They were chosen from published specifications,
            maker product pages and data sheets, shipping and warranty terms, recall records and
            independent coverage, and the claims on this page are limited to what those sources
            support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from design. A dedicated hack squat is a single inclined sled with shoulder
            pads, built for one lift. A combo shares a sled with a leg press. A pendulum squat swings
            the pads on a lever arm in an arc. A hack press, as Steelflex uses the term, is a
            commercial-style plate-loaded lever machine for the same movement. For each we looked for
            models the maker listed as on sale on 19 October 2026, preferably sold on Amazon US too.
            The independent sources were Garage Gym Reviews' review of the Titan combo, Iron
            Company's listing for the Body-Solid, indexed Amazon listings for the Titan combo and
            the Body-Solid, and, for the machines added on 9 November 2026, Body-Solid's data sheet,
            GMWD's store and retailer listings.
          </p>
          <p>
            Independent coverage of dedicated home hack squats is thin, and that shows on the cards.
            Titan's dedicated machine and the Bells of Steel combo carry the makers' figures,
            labelled as such. Where sources did disagree, we left the figure off: the Titan combo's
            capacity (1,000 lb from Titan and the reviewer, 875 lb in an Amazon spec table), the
            Body-Solid's carriage weight (60 lb from Body-Solid, 80 lb from Iron Company), the
            Body-Solid lever squat's width (43 or 47 inches), and the GMWD PS01's capacity and parts
            warranty, which retailers and GMWD give differently.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every brand on this page was checked against the CPSC recall database by recall title,
            manufacturer and recall description on 19 October 2026. There is no recall in the
            database under "hack squat". Titan had a 2018 recall alert for weight-lifting safety
            straps, a rack accessory, and the database's one leg press recall is <a href="/cybex-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Cybex</a>'s 2012 recall of
            its commercial model 16110 plate-loaded leg press. We found no recalls for Bells of
            Steel, Body-Solid, GMWD, Force USA or Steelflex.
          </p>
          <p>
            We deliberately left several machines out. The Force USA Compact Leg Press &amp; Hack
            Squat (about $999, a 30-degree sled with a 38 lb start), the Bells of Steel Pandemonium
            (about $1,750) and the Steelflex PLHP (about $3,380) are sold on their makers' stores or
            through dealers, not on Amazon, so they are discussed below but not carded. Bells of
            Steel's Iso Leg Press and Hack Squat, which Garage Gym Reviews lists at $2,999, was
            judged not worth the premium by that reviewer. Force USA's Ultimate 45 Degree combo ($1,899) and Major Fitness's AH1
            ($1,399.99) are on sale but we could not check the makers' figures beyond capacity.
            RitFit sells a rack-mounted hack squat attachment and a 45-degree combo, and Mikolo's
            combos were out of stock on its store on the day. Bolt Fitness's Outlaw combo, which
            Garage Gym Reviews liked, was reported sold out in that review, and we did not confirm
            that it is back in stock.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Dedicated, Combo, Pendulum and Hack Press</h2>
<p>
            All four designs guide your squat and support your back, but they move the load in
            different ways.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Dedicated hack squat sled.</strong> A single inclined sled with shoulder pads
            and a back pad, sliding on rails, with a fixed footplate. Titan's runs at 35 degrees on
            linear bearings. Because it does one job, a dedicated sled can be shorter, lighter and
            cheaper than a combo — Titan's is 190 lb and 60.5 inches deep by Titan's figures — and
            there are no pads to swap. Choose one if you want the hack squat and do not care about
            the leg press.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Leg press/hack squat combo.</strong> A 45-degree sled (30 on the Force USA
            Compact) with two sets of pads: face one way to leg press, the other to hack squat. It
            gives two lifts and calf raises for one footprint, which is why it dominates home
            ranges. The compromise is that one angle and one set of rails serve both lifts: Garage
            Gym Reviews said the hack squat on Titan's combo felt heavier than on most commercial
            machines, and both modes share the same footplates and lockouts.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Pendulum squat.</strong> The pads and footplate are fixed to a lever that swings
            in an arc around a pivot rather than sliding on rails. The arc changes how hard the
            lift feels through the range, and many lifters find it easier on the lower back than a
            sled. Bells of Steel's Pandemonium adds a counterbalance arm to offset the lever's own
            weight and can be set up for calf raises and a Viking press.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Hack press (lever).</strong> Steelflex's PLHP is a commercial-style plate-loaded
            lever machine with sealed bearings at the pivots. Lever machines of this kind are common
            in commercial gyms and are built for heavy, repeated use, but they are long and costly.
          </p>
          <p>
            A Smith machine can do a version of the hack squat with your feet set forward of the
            bar, and a belt squat or a
            <a href="/best-safety-squat-bars/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">safety squat bar</a>
            in a rack are other ways to squat with less stress on the shoulders and upper back. Our
            guide to <a href="/best-smith-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Smith machines</a>
            compares bar paths if you want one machine for squats and presses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sled-angle" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Sled Angle and Why the Same Plates Feel Different</h2>
<p>
            The angle of a hack squat's rails is the biggest single difference between machines,
            and it changes how much of the load you actually lift.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The plates.</strong> On an inclined sled only part of the plates' weight acts
            along the rails. Ignoring friction, the share is the sine of the angle: about 71% at 45
            degrees, about 57% at 35 degrees and 50% at 30 degrees. Load 180 lb of plates on a sled,
            and you push roughly 127 lb along a 45-degree path, 103 lb along a 35-degree path and 90 lb
            along a 30-degree one, plus the share of the sled itself. That is simplified physics from
            the stated angles, not a measurement.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Your body weight.</strong> In a hack squat, unlike a leg press, your own body
            rides up and down the rails with the sled. So on top of the plates, you lift a share of
            your body weight — a larger share on a steeper sled. On a 45-degree sled a 180 lb lifter
            moves about 71% of their body weight along the path as well as the plates, which is one
            reason hack squats feel harder than a leg press with the same plates, and why the Titan
            combo felt heavy to Garage Gym Reviews' tester.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Sled angle</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Machines here</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Share of load along the rails</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">45°</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Titan combo (and most combos)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 71%</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">35°</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Titan Linear Hack Squat</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 57%</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">30°</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Force USA Compact</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">50%</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Arc</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">GMWD PS01, Body-Solid lever squat, Pandemonium, Steelflex PLHP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Changes through the rep</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>How angle changes the lift.</strong> A steeper sled keeps your torso more
            upright and puts more of your body weight in the lift, which suits lifters who want a
            demanding squat pattern. A shallower sled leans you back further, takes more of your
            body weight off, and is easier to learn and easier on the knees for many people, though
            you need more plates. Neither is better; the useful rule is that numbers on one machine
            tell you nothing about another. Log your sets per machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Foot position.</strong> On any hack squat, feet higher on the platform shift work
            toward the glutes and hamstrings and lower feet toward the quads, which is why a large
            platform matters. Titan gives its combo's hack squat footplate as 26 by 22 inches and
            its dedicated machine's as 25.25 by 23.25 inches; Bells of Steel gives its Pandemonium a
            21.6 inch square platform.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shoulder-pads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Shoulder Pads, Back Pad and Fit</h2>
<p>
            In a hack squat the shoulder pads carry the whole load into your body, so their size,
            spacing and adjustment matter more than on almost any other machine.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Pad spacing.</strong> Titan gives its combo's shoulder pads a 7.5 inch
            spread and 4.5 by 8 inch pads. Narrow pads can press on the neck or collarbone of broad
            lifters; wide pads can slip off narrow shoulders. If you are well above or below average
            build, look for the spread on the spec sheet or ask the maker.</li>
            <li><strong>Pad thickness.</strong> Body-Solid gives the GLPH1100B 4-inch-thick
            contoured back and shoulder pads, the most detailed pad figure here. Thicker pads
            spread the load and are more comfortable under heavy sets.</li>
            <li><strong>Height adjustment.</strong> The distance from the footplate to the shoulder
            pads has to suit your height, or you will start too deep or be unable to stand fully.
            Force USA says the Compact's hack squat pad adjusts both horizontally and vertically and
            gives a 5 ft 0 in to 6 ft 8 in user range; Bells of Steel says the Pandemonium's three
            peg levels suit lifters up to about 6 ft 5 in; Titan's dedicated machine has an
            adjustable backrest. Garage Gym Reviews thought the Titan combo's hack squat would not
            suit lifters 6 ft 3 in or taller.</li>
            <li><strong>Handles.</strong> Most machines put the lockout levers on the handles beside
            the shoulder pads, so you release and re-engage the sled with your hands in place. Check
            that you can reach them comfortably at the bottom of your range.</li>
            <li><strong>User weight limits.</strong> Steelflex's data sheet gives the PLHP a
            maximum user weight of 120 kg, about 265 lb, and Force USA gives the Compact 320 lb. Your
            body weight is part of the load in a hack squat, so check this figure as carefully as the
            plate rating.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If shoulder or neck discomfort is likely, a pad wrap or a folded towel helps some
            lifters, but persistent pain is a reason to stop and get advice, not to add padding and
            push on. A belt squat or a leg press loads the legs without loading the shoulders.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety-stops" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Safety Stops, Lockouts and Training Alone</h2>
<p>
            Hack squats are popular in home gyms because they let you train to near failure
            without a spotter. That depends on two different safety systems.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Lockouts (racking positions).</strong> The levers that hold the sled at the
            top. Titan and Body-Solid give their combos three, and Force USA gives the Compact
            eight. Titan's dedicated machine uses flip-in, flip-out racking handles that you work
            without leaving the machine, per Titan. More positions let you start from a height that
            suits you.</li>
            <li><strong>Bottom safety stops.</strong> A hard limit on how far the sled can descend.
            Force USA describes stops built into the Compact's travel path; Titan says its dedicated
            machine lets you set start and stop points to match your range of motion; the
            Pandemonium uses a spotter arm, per Bells of Steel. Set the bottom stop just below the
            deepest point you can reach with your back flat on the pad and your heels down, so a
            failed rep ends on the stop.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A few habits make solo hack squats safer. Practise releasing and re-engaging the
            lockouts with the empty sled until it is automatic. Load plates evenly, one on each
            side at a time where there are two horns, so the sled does not twist. Do not hold your
            breath through long sets; a hard squat raises blood pressure sharply, which is one
            reason anyone with high blood pressure or a heart condition should get advice first.
            Strip the plates when you finish and leave the sled on its lowest lockout, as Garage
            Gym Reviews suggests for the Titan combo, so a child cannot release it. Recovery after a
            heavy leg session is covered in our guide to
            <a href="/best-recovery-tools-for-weightlifters/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">recovery tools for weightlifters</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Footprint, Plate Storage and Floor Loading</h2>
<p>
            Hack squats are shorter than most leg presses but still take a good stretch of floor,
            and a loaded one is heavy. These are the makers' assembled figures, rounded.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Machine</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Footprint</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Machine weight</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Titan Linear Hack Squat</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">46.75 x 60.5 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">190 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Force USA Compact (not carded)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">52 x 65 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not published</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Titan combo</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">84 x 40 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">325 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Body-Solid GLPH1100B</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">96 x 34 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">317 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bells of Steel combo</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">87 x 37.5 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">322 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">GMWD PS01</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 63 x 43 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">287 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Body-Solid GSCL360</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">62 x 43 or 47 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">170 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bells of Steel Pandemonium (not carded)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 88 x 48.5 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">380 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Steelflex PLHP (not carded)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 99 x 59 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 377 lb</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Height and working room.</strong> Most hack squats are under five feet tall,
            but the Pandemonium stands about 88 inches at full extension by Bells of Steel's figure,
            because its upright doubles as a pull-up station, so check its height against your
            ceiling and any beams. Leave room to step in under the pads, load plates from the sides
            and, on a combo, swap pads from the end; three feet around the frame is a sensible
            plan, and Force USA's manual recommends 3 to 5 feet around the Compact.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Plate storage.</strong> Titan's combo has two storage posts, the Pandemonium two
            storage pegs and Titan's dedicated machine none listed beyond its loading sleeves.
            Storage on the frame keeps plates close but adds weight to the machine's feet; a
            separate plate tree spreads the load and keeps the loading area clear.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Floor loading.</strong> On a ground-floor slab this is rarely a concern.
            Upstairs, do the arithmetic. Titan lists its dedicated hack squat at 190 lb on a 46.75 by
            60.5 inch footprint, about 19.6 square feet. Add 360 lb of plates and a 200 lb lifter,
            and the total is about 750 lb, or roughly 38 lb per square foot averaged over the frame —
            our arithmetic from Titan's stated inputs, not a measurement. That is close to the 40 lb
            per square foot that the International Residential Code uses as the design live load for
            living areas (30 for sleeping rooms), and the load is concentrated on the frame's feet.
            As general guidance rather than engineering advice: put a hack squat on the ground floor
            or a slab where you can, keep stored plates elsewhere if it must go upstairs, and ask a
            structural engineer about older houses or long joist spans. Rubber flooring under the
            frame and loading area protects the floor; our
            <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym flooring guide</a>
            and <a href="/best-lifting-platforms/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifting platform guide</a>
            compare the options.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Assembly.</strong> Titan describes its dedicated machine as a bolt-together steel
            frame, Force USA says most people build the Compact in about 90 minutes, and an owner
            review on Bells of Steel's page describes building the Pandemonium alone over a few hours. We have not assembled these machines. Build
            on the spot the machine will live, with a second person for the sled and rails, and
            check that the sled runs smoothly through its full travel before you load it. Our guide
            to <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">assembling a treadmill</a>
            covers tools and two-person lifting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            A hack squat is a specialist machine. For some readers, other equipment does the job
            better for the money or the space.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>First home gyms.</strong> A
            <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squat rack</a>
            and barbell, plus a <a href="/best-weight-benches/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">bench</a>,
            train the legs and everything else. Most coaches would buy those first.</li>
            <li><strong>People who want leg loading without shoulder loading.</strong> A
            <a href="/best-leg-press-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">leg press</a>
            puts the load through your feet with your back supported and nothing on your
            shoulders.</li>
            <li><strong>Glute-focused training.</strong> A
            <a href="/best-hip-thrust-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">hip thrust machine</a>
            targets the glutes more directly than a hack squat.</li>
            <li><strong>Small rooms and upstairs floors.</strong>
            <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Adjustable dumbbells</a>
            for split squats and lunges, or a compact
            <a href="/best-home-gym-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym machine</a>,
            take a fraction of the floor and load.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are still planning the room, our
            <a href="/home-workout-equipment-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home workout equipment reviews</a>
            set leg machines alongside the other big purchases, and the
            <a href="/home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Home Gym hub</a>
            lists every guide in this section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best hack squat machine for a home gym?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a dedicated hack squat, Titan's Plate-Loaded Linear Hack Squat, a 35-degree sled on linear bearings for about $975, though its figures are Titan's own. For a machine that also leg presses, Body-Solid's GLPH1100B, whose capacity, lockouts and footprint Body-Solid and a dealer agree on; Titan's combo is the best-reviewed but is sold on Titan's store rather than on Amazon. For a short room, the Force USA Compact at 30 degrees.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a hack squat or leg press better for a home gym?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on what you want to train. A hack squat loads you through the shoulders and moves your body with the sled, so it feels closer to a squat and is harder with the same plates. A leg press keeps your body still and loads only through the feet. A combo does both in one footprint, which is why most home buyers choose one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What angle should a hack squat machine be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no single right angle. Most combos run at about 45 degrees; Titan's dedicated sled is 35 and Force USA's Compact 30. A steeper sled puts more of your body weight and the plates into the lift; a shallower one leans you back and feels lighter. Compare numbers only on the same machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does a hack squat feel heavier than a leg press?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a hack squat your body rides the sled, so you lift a share of your own weight as well as the plates: about 71% of it on a 45-degree sled, ignoring friction. On a leg press your body stays still. Garage Gym Reviews also found the hack squat on Titan's combo heavier than on most commercial machines.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much space does a home hack squat machine need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Titan lists its dedicated hack squat at 46.75 by 60.5 inches, the GMWD PS01 pendulum is about 63 by 43, and the leg press combos are 84 to 96 inches long. Allow about three feet around the frame to step in, load plates and swap pads. Pendulum and lever machines swing upward as you stand, so check the ceiling too.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a hack squat machine safe to use alone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is one of the safer ways to squat hard alone, provided you set the bottom stop just below your deepest safe position every session, practise the lockout levers with an empty sled, load plates evenly and stay within the user weight limit. Talk to a qualified professional first if you have a knee, back, neck or heart condition or high blood pressure.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Hack Squat Machine</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these turns a heavy, single-purpose machine into one that disappoints, does not fit or is less safe than it should be.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing hack squat numbers across angles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The same plates on a 45-degree, 35-degree and 30-degree sled are different efforts, and your body weight adds more on a steeper sled. Judge a machine by its fit and feel, and track progress on your own machine.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the user weight limit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">In a hack squat your body is part of the load. Steelflex gives the PLHP a 265 lb maximum user weight and Force USA the Compact 320 lb. Check the user limit as carefully as the plate rating, and ask the maker if it is not published.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying without checking shoulder-pad fit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Pad spread, thickness and the distance from footplate to pads decide whether a machine fits your build and height. Look for those figures, or the maker's height range, and be wary of combos reviewers found short for tall lifters.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the bottom stop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Lockouts hold the sled at the top; only the bottom stop catches a failed rep. Set it just below the deepest point you can reach with your back flat on the pad, every session, and leave the sled on its lowest lockout when you finish.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

